#!/bin/bash
# plan-gap <plan.md> — cold-read a saved plan and list what a fresh session would
# still have to ask. Run this before /clear: non-empty output is the quality you'd
# lose by clearing. Fix the gaps in the plan, re-run, then clear.
#
# Why: measured 2026-08-09 on a real plan. A cleared session had 13 blocking
# questions; only 2 were missing rationale. The rest were identifiers — commit SHA,
# repo, branch, run commands, type shapes, current state.
set -euo pipefail

[[ $# -eq 1 ]] || { echo "usage: plan-gap <plan.md>" >&2; exit 2; }
[[ -f "$1" ]] || { echo "plan-gap: no such file: $1" >&2; exit 2; }
plan="$(cd "$(dirname "$1")" && pwd)/$(basename "$1")"

read -r -d '' PROMPT <<'EOF' || true
You have been handed the plan below and nothing else — no prior conversation, no
repo access yet. You must execute its first phase.

List ONLY the concrete things you would have to ask the author or rediscover
before you could start, because the plan does not state them. Be specific. Group
under these headings, omitting any that are empty:
ANCHOR-MISSING (repo/branch/commit SHA) / RUN-IT-MISSING (build, test, boot,
ports, profiles) / SHAPES-MISSING (types, enums, vocabularies named but not
defined) / DECISIONS-NOT-RECORDED (conclusions without the eliminations) /
ALREADY-TRUE-UNKNOWN (existing branches, merged prerequisites, known bugs).
Max 12 bullets. If the plan is genuinely self-contained, reply exactly: NO GAPS.

--- PLAN ---
EOF

cd "$(dirname "$plan")"
claude -p "${PROMPT}
$(cat "$plan")"
